<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florence Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1296">1296</a>–<a href="/years/1436">1436</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Santa Maria del Fiore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> Cathedral, the Cattedrale di Santa Maria del Fiore, is the cathedral of Florence, begun in 1296 to designs by Arnolfo di Cambio and consecrated in 1436. Its octagonal dome by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a>, completed in 1436, was the largest built since antiquity, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s bell tower and the Baptistery the cathedral forms the centre of the historic city, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral.jpg" alt="Florence Cathedral" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Campbell-Hall · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cattedrale_di_Santa_Maria_del_Fiore_%E2%80%93_Il_Duomo_di_Firenze.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Santa Maria del Fiore was built on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>'s second cathedral, dedicated to Saint Reparata. According to Giovanni Villani's Nuova Cronica, the old building was crumbling and too small for the growing city, and other Tuscan cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and Siena among them, were rebuilding their cathedrals. The city council approved Arnolfo di Cambio's design in 1294, and Cardinal Valeriana, the first papal legate ever sent to Florence, laid the first stone on 9 September 1296. The project was to last 140 years.</p><p>After Arnolfo died in 1302 work slowed for almost 50 years. The discovery of the relics of Saint Zenobius in 1330 gave it new impetus, and in 1331 the wool merchants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arte-della-lana">Arte della Lana</a>, took over its patronage. In 1334 it appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>, who began the campanile. He died on 8 January 1337, and Andrea Pisano continued until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> halted work in 1348.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The cathedral is a basilica in the shape of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cross, with a wide central nave of four square bays and an aisle on either side. Its trilobate apses were meant to evoke a flower, in homage to Fiorenza, the city of flowers. It is 153 m long and 38 m wide, and its dome rises 114.5 m. The exterior is faced with polychrome marble panels in green, pink and white.</p><p>The dome was planned from the start without external buttresses. In 1367 the model of Neri di Fioravanti was chosen over a rival's, a decision described as one of the first events of the Italian Renaissance, since Gothic flying buttresses were thought ugly and were forbidden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. The original façade was dismantled in 1587 and 1588 on the orders of Grand Duke Francesco I de' Medici. The present Gothic Revival façade by Emilio De Fabris was built from 1876 to 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The interior is Late Gothic and bare, which suits the austerity of the mendicant orders. Because public funds paid for the cathedral, its notable works honour Florentine heroes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> appears in a fresco of 1465 by Domenico di Michelino, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-uccello">Paolo Uccello</a> and Andrea del Castagno painted the condottieri Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawkwood">John Hawkwood</a> and Niccolò da Tolentino as riders in 1436 and 1456. In 1475 the astronomer Paolo dal Pozzo Toscanelli pierced a hole in the dome to make a meridian line.</p><p>Cracks in the dome were seen even before it was finished. In 1694 two major cracks were measured at up to 29 mm, and a commission proposed iron belts, but after long debate the dome was left as it was. Excavations from 1965 to 1974 uncovered Roman houses and the ruins of Santa Reparata, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a>'s simple tomb lies in the crypt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Florence Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fifteenth century Filippo Brunelleschi’s dome of <b>Florence Cathedral</b>, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of Donatello and the painting of Masaccio launched the artistic Renaissance, followed by Sandro Botticelli, Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, whose David was set up outside the Palazzo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its influence runs from Brunelleschi's dome of <b>Santa Maria del Fiore</b> in Florence, completed in 1436, through Bernini's Santa Maria Assunta in Ariccia (1664), St. Hedwig's Cathedral in Berlin, Belle Isle House in England and the Rotunda of Mosta in Malta.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Members of the Pazzi conspiracy, a plot to displace the Medici as rulers of Florence, stabbed Giuliano de' Medici to death in <b>Florence Cathedral</b> and wounded his brother Lorenzo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-26">26 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
